// A hash-based identification. A secret key has a public identity, the MiMC hash of the
// key, published once. To identify against a fresh public challenge, the prover shows both
// that it knows the key behind the identity and that it can bind that key to the challenge,
// each through the one-way permutation. Neither the identity nor the response reveals the
// key, and because the response is tied to the challenge, a transcript cannot be replayed
// against a different one. It is the login a zero-knowledge identity proves. Run: zkolang
// id_proof.zkl
include "hash.zkl";

secret sk;

input identity;
input challenge;
input response;

// The public identity is the one-way hash of the secret key, so knowing a preimage of it
// is knowing the key.
assert permute(sk) == identity;

// The response binds the key to this challenge. Only the holder of the key can produce it,
// and it discloses nothing about the key on its own.
assert permute(sk + challenge) == response;
